swer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-57954fbb-365e-514c-9455-fed7a902987d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-57954fbb-365e-514c-9455-fed7a902987d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-57954fbb-365e-514c-9455-fed7a902987d" :answer/id #uuid "57954fbb-365e-514c-9455-fed7a902987d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-22.63" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f9693f01-1b7b-566f-8d4a-ee5e7f15cd38" :question/id #uuid "f9693f01-1b7b-566f-8d4a-ee5e7f15cd38" :question/math-academy-id "q-128430" :question/problem "$(-1.6)\\cdot (-4.3)=$" :question/worked-solution "We have the product of two negative numbers, which gives a positive number.\n\n$$\n(-1.6)\\cdot (-4.3)=1.6\\cdot 4.3=6.88.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-feb44731-205d-5332-884e-4272d2e37e6b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-feb44731-205d-5332-884e-4272d2e37e6b" :answer-field/id #uuid "feb44731-205d-5332-884e-4272d2e37e6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5eec4216-dacd-5307-b6df-b8a602275f81" :answer-field/choices ["answer-06cbbd89-6b21-5af3-8655-50d24c22d57d" "answer-52cbaa17-3835-5e42-98ba-4499290c8540" "answer-cff80ab7-84cd-5c8c-8e19-4ee3ed2405ee" "answer-5eec4216-dacd-5307-b6df-b8a602275f81" "answer-bc465659-da43-5354-91cc-749eb7ba8bb0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-06cbbd89-6b21-5af3-8655-50d24c22d57d" :answer/id #uuid "06cbbd89-6b21-5af3-8655-50d24c22d57d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5.98" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-52cbaa17-3835-5e42-98ba-4499290c8540" :answer/id #uuid "52cbaa17-3835-5e42-98ba-4499290c8540" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-7.02" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cff80ab7-84cd-5c8c-8e19-4ee3ed2405ee" :answer/id #uuid "cff80ab7-84cd-5c8c-8e19-4ee3ed2405ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6.12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5eec4216-dacd-5307-b6df-b8a602275f81" :answer/id #uuid "5eec4216-dacd-5307-b6df-b8a602275f81" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6.88" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bc465659-da43-5354-91cc-749eb7ba8bb0" :answer/id #uuid "bc465659-da43-5354-91cc-749eb7ba8bb0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7.12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186180195 :question/problem "$(-4)\\cdot (-5.1)=$" :question/worked-solution "We have the product of two negative numbers, which gives a positive number.\n\n$$\n(-4)\\cdot (-5.1)=4\\cdot 5.1=20.4.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186178714 :answer-field/correct 17592186178719 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186180772 :tutorial/content "When we multiply two negative numbers, the answer is always a positive number. \n\nAs an example, let's calculate the product\n\n$$\n(-2) \\cdot (-8).\n$$\n\nHere, we have a product of two negative numbers, $\\color{red}-2$ and ${\\color{red}-8}.$ The product will be positive. You can think of it as though the two minus signs cancel each other out:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-)\\,\\cdot \\,(-)\\,=\\,(+)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have\n![](images/33/33e5a88fe38fb96c92b9ecd4599825d2ae99b96201a734fc3719c0f822b20a05.svg)\nNow, to compute the number in the box, we multiply $2$ by $8$ without any signs: $2 \\cdot 8 = 16$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-2)} \\cdot {\\color{red}(-8)} = {\\color{blue} 16}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186180655 :tutorial/content "Here, we will prove that the product of two negative numbers is positive. Please **don't worry** if you don't fully understand all the details. Just follow along as best you can. You can circle back later if needed. \n\nTo explain why the product of two negatives is positive, we need to state some properties of numbers that we use regularly:\n\n- Subtracting a number is the same as adding its opposite:\n\n$$\na - b = a + (-b)\n$$\n\nFor example, $2-3 = 2 + (-3).$\n\n- The **commutative law**: For any numbers $a$ and $b,$ we have:\n\n$$\na\\cdot b = b\\cdot a\n$$\n\n \nFor example, $2\\cdot 3 = 3\\cdot 2.$\n\n- The **distributive law**: For any numbers $a, b$ and $c,$ we have:\n\n$$\na\\cdot (b+c) = a\\cdot b + a\\cdot c\n$$\n\n \nFor example, $2\\cdot (3+4) = 2\\cdot 3 + 2\\cdot 4.$\n\nLet's now prove the following result:\n\n$$\n(-1) \\cdot (-1) = 1\n$$\n\nWe start with the following true equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is true because any number multiplied by zero equals zero.\n\nNow, since $0=1 -1,$ we can write the zero on the left-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n(-1) \\cdot (1-1) = 0\n$$\n\nSubtracting a positive is the same as adding a negative. Therefore:\n\n$$\n(-1) \\cdot \\big(1+ (-1)\\big) = 0\n$$\n\nWe now apply the distributive law:\n\n$$\n(-1) \\cdot 1+ (-1)\\cdot (-1) = 0\n$$\n\nApplying the commutative law to the first term on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n1\\cdot (-1)+ (-1)\\cdot (-1) = 0\n$$\n\nThe number $1\\cdot (-1)$ equals $-1,$ since this just means one copy of $-1.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n-1+ (-1)\\cdot (-1) = 0\n$$\n\nFinally, adding $1$ to both sides of the equation, we finally arrive at:\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot (-1) = 1\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179869 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
